  "image_name": "0d00c2eda8cb66d01dbd73b3799ae88a.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the structure of a ferroelectric transistor and highlights the key internal layers that control its behavior. At the top, a generic gate stack is shown sitting above a semiconductor substrate with source/drain contacts. The main emphasis is on two parts of this stack: a thin interfacial layer between the channel region and the upper films, and the thicker ferroelectric layer above it. One schematic isolates the interfacial layer, while another points out the ferroelectric layer within the complete gate structure, making clear that device performance depends on both the interface and the ferroelectric film rather than the gate alone.\n\nThe lower part compares three visible ways the ferroelectric layer can be implemented. One appears as a superlattice made of alternating thin layers on a dielectric, another as dispersed ferroelectric nanocrystals embedded in a dielectric matrix, and the third as a nanosheet-style stacked geometry contacted by the gate. Together, these drawings convey that ferroelectric transistors can be engineered through different material organizations and architectures, but all rely on integrating a ferroelectric region with a gate stack over the substrate and source/drain regions.",
